The '''Banc Balboa Tower''' ({{lang|ca|Torre Banc Balboa}}) is a 46-storey office tower at 100 Carretera Raúl Jame in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]. Completed in 1982, it was the first major skyscraper built during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] and an early symbol of the boom. The tower serves as the corporate headquarters of [[Banc Balboa]], the largest bank in Balboa.

The tower was damaged by a fire during the real-estate asset crisis of the early 1990s — its construction had been financed by the same lending practices that caused the crisis — and was refurbished under [[Grup Financer Central]]'s ownership in the mid-1990s.

== Architecture ==

[[File:Banc Balboa Tower.png|thumb|right|Banc Balboa Tower in daylight, viewed from the Districte Financer]]

The Banc Balboa Tower consists of a curved glass office shaft rising from a broader base, topped by a rectangular crown section. The lower portion of the tower contains the main lobby, banking halls, conference facilities, and building services. The curved glass portion above the base contains approximately 40 to 42 regular office floors, while the upper rectangular section houses mechanical equipment, communications facilities, and executive or observation floors. A further 3 to 5 floors in the upper crown and mechanical zone complete the tower's profile.

The tower's footprint is approximately 2,000 to 2,400 m², with approximate dimensions of 54 to 58 metres by 38 to 43 metres. The typical gross office floor plate is approximately 1,750 to 2,050 m². The building's curved façade distinguishes it from the later rectangular towers of the Districte Financer and gives it a distinctive profile on the Sant Cristòfor waterfront skyline.

