== History ==

=== Construction ===

The Banc Balboa Tower was commissioned by [[Banc Balboa]] in 1979 as the bank's new corporate headquarters. Construction began the same year and the tower was completed in 1982. The tower was the first major commercial skyscraper built in Sant Cristòfor since the [[Zong]] era and marked the beginning of the wave of tower construction that would define the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] of the mid-1980s.

The tower's construction was financed by Banc Balboa's own lending operations — the same absorbent lending practices that would later cause the real-estate crisis. The bank's decision to build a landmark headquarters tower was driven by the post-reunification economic boom and the growing demand for premium office space in the Districte Financer.

=== Real-estate crisis and refurbishment ===

The tower was damaged by a fire during the real-estate asset crisis of the early 1990s. The crisis, triggered by the collapse of speculative real-estate lending, affected Banc Balboa severely — the bank had financed both its own headquarters and many of the bubble-era developments that defaulted. The tower's construction loans became non-performing, and the building's value collapsed alongside the broader real-estate market. The fire, which broke out in the upper floors during a period of reduced maintenance and security, caused significant damage to the crown section and mechanical levels.

[[Grup Financer Central]] acquired Banc Balboa and its assets, including the tower, during the restructuring of the financial sector. The tower was refurbished in the mid-1990s under Grup Financer Central's ownership, with repairs to the façade, mechanical systems, and interior spaces. The building has since served as Banc Balboa's corporate headquarters under successive ownership.

== Use ==

The Banc Balboa Tower serves as the corporate headquarters of [[Banc Balboa]]. The tower houses the bank's executive offices, corporate departments, and client-facing services. The lower floors contain banking halls and customer service facilities, while the upper floors contain executive offices and the mechanical and communications equipment that crown the building.

